{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The first of your grain.</b> It has no measure, except that its gift is recognizable, so that a person does not make his entire threshing-floor into terumah. And according to one opinion of the Sages, a single grain exempts the entire pile. However, our teachers assigned it measures: an evil eye — one part in sixty; a goodly eye — one in forty; middling — one in fifty. And they supported it from Scripture: one in sixty, as it is written ״ששית האיפה מחמר החטים״, and an eifah is three se'ah, so a sixth of an eifah is half a se'ah, and a kor is thirty se'ah — so half a se'ah for thirty se'ah, and that is one in sixty. A goodly eye — one in forty, as it is written ״וששיתם את האיפה מחמר החטים ומחמר השעורים״, and וששיתם implies two sixths, plus the sixth of an eifah mentioned above — that makes three sixths, which amounts to a se'ah and a half. And it is written מחמר החטים ומחמר השעורים, which are two korin that amount to sixty se'ah — so half a se'ah for twenty se'ah, and that is one in forty. Middling — one in fifty, as it is written regarding the spoil of Midian: one portion taken from the fifty, and Scripture calls it terumah, and also the language of תרומה is two from a hundred." "<b>And the first of the shearing of your flock you shall give him.</b> In the chapter ראשית הגז it is explained that it requires enough for a gift — that he give him wool sufficient to make a belt."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The phrase עין רעה in this context denotes the most miserly measure of terumah, per the Sages' usage in Mishnah Terumot 4:3."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:09.079199779Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}